A1. The way the game of football started. ~ KICKOFF
A7. These were mustered between blankets during the halves. ~ HOTDOGS
A13. The beginning of the end. ~ EN
A14. This was carried out from the huddle. ~ IDEA
A15. The kind of Americans a colored team makes. ~ AFRO
A16. A split atom. ~ OM
A17. Most games start on this. ~ DOT
A18. Here is where you get the football habit. ~ TOG
A19. One of the rooters. ~ HOG
A22. What the team did between quarters. ~ RETIRED
A26. Popular breed of dog. ~ HOT
A28. A tooter for the home team. ~ OBOE
A30. Two down and this to go. ~ SEVEN
A31. You'll get a kick out of this one. ~ PUNT
A32. Heaps. ~ LOADS
A34. Let's take a little rest and play golf. ~ TEE
A35. A ridiculous play. ~ FARCE
A36. Some English players run like this. ~ ELL
A37. You can work up a good plot with this. ~ HOE
A39. You were a better humorist than we are, Bill. ~ NYE
A41. One of the outstanding figures in football. ~ TEN
A42. Topsy's side-kick. ~ EVA
A43. This twenty yard line is often wet. ~ TOW
A44. A scrimmage in the pressroom. ~ PI
A46. What the losing team failed to do. ~ BEAT
A47. What the team did the day they lost. ~ RUED
A49. For the losers it means pity, for the winners triumph. ~ AH
A51. 1/2 wit. ~ IDIOT
A53. Positively the last appearance of Hor. 42. ~ EVA
A55. Ripping football, don'tchaknow. ~ RUGBY
A57. What the winners do with pride. ~ GLOW
A58. The only thing that can make some players smart enough to run. ~ STING
A60. It is hard to keep this up to standard. ~ GOLD
A61. A Japanese copper. ~ SEN
A62. One of the players slipped on this. ~ SWEATER
A64. A little ant made a tyrant out of this one. ~ TYR
A65. Dis Greek beta de gamma. ~ PHI
A66. Broadcast receiver. ~ EAR
A68. The most exasperating word in the English language. ~ IF
A70. This used to work magic. ~ WAND
A72. A Japanese goal. ~ ASIA
A73. What the game is when in action. ~ ON
A74. The side that won at 0-0. ~ NEITHER
A75. To whom the players go for drilling. ~ DENTIST

D1. The trainer should look into this. ~ KEYHOLE
D2. Not out of bounds. ~ IN
D3. The little brother of the mascot. ~ KID
D4. This hangs around the rooters. ~ ODOR
D5. The visiting team gets these pleasures. ~ FETES
D6. This is beginning to be fat, poor thing. ~ FA
D7. It always starts a laugh. ~ HA
D8. When football players like to eat. ~ OFTEN
D9. What some of the players did on defeat. ~ TROD
D10. Watch for this one. His number is K9. ~ DOG
D11. Forward pass! ~ GO
D12. What the co-eds were with the football captain. ~ SMITTEN
D20. An ancient Greek coin. ~ OBOL
D21. The end of a perfect play. ~ GOAL
D23. A huddle for two (3 words). ~ TETEATETE
D24. This finishes the narrative. ~ IVE
D25. The man who came back. ~ REENTRANT
D26. Make sore. ~ HURT
D27. —upon a time. ~ ONCE
D29. So-o-o-o-o, this is a win. ~ ED
D31. A supporter of college sports. ~ PA
D33. This was placed on the back-yard line. ~ SHEET
D35. What football mishaps are now. ~ FEWER
D38. Something hardboiled in a dago restaurant. (plur.) ~ OVA
D40. Thou art the one. ~ YOU
D44. The skin you love to touch-down. ~ PIGSKIN
D45. No action. ~ IDLE
D46. Good legs for a snapper-back. ~ BOW
D48. What they did to excavate the players at the bottom of the heap. ~ DUG
D49. The way the winners played. ~ ABLY
D50. The first stop after getting on the water wagon. ~ HYDRANT
D52. It is not hard to get this charged. ~ ION
D54. A traveler's by-word. ~ VIA
D56. Half of New York City is here. ~ GOT
D58. A bunch of rooters for the home team. ~ SWINE
D59. These make a wild game. ~ GEESE
D62. This easterner had his head crowned. ~ SHAH
D63. What the hero was showered with after the game. ~ RAIN
D65. It carries little weight in Troy. (abbr.) ~ PWT
D67. Cat meat. ~ RAT
D69. Husbands have a hard time with these males. ~ FE
D71. He has a lot of inside information about the players. (abbr.) ~ DR
D72. It helped to bring out a big crowd. ~ AD
D73. This one was given a good break in the game. (Latin) ~ OS


